Cell[TextData[{
 "An option ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<LengthPreserving\>\""], "InlineFormula", ExpressionUUID->
  "c311b0d0-2516-a042-85bd-510ad3674572"],
 " was created because at some point one might want to expand the system to \
allow it to jump to a specific point on the tape instead of using relative \
offsets, and that would only work with length-preserving rules, so it would \
be good to be able to check. Another reason the option defaults to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["True",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:ref/True"]], "InlineFormula", ExpressionUUID->
  "d5d2f76b-4ec6-f547-8a7a-1ebf7ada1454"],
 " is that there is a fairly straightforward way to compile oblivious ring \
machines to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula", ExpressionUUID->
  "693b6c19-75be-b845-affe-778364d7147a"],
 "-ary cyclic tag, which is a useful compilation pathway, but it only works \
with an oblivious ring machine that preserves the length of the ring. The \
option does not currently check that the rules are in fact length preserving, \
but in the future it could. For this reason, it is best to set ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"\"\<LengthPreserving\>\"", 
   RowBox[{" ", 
    RowBox[{"->", " "}]}], 
   ButtonBox["False",
    BaseStyle->"Link",
    ButtonData->"paclet:ref/False"]}]], "InlineFormula", ExpressionUUID->
  "fa80c3da-1c80-964d-bf7e-3272a97dd620"],
 " when using rules that change the length, even though the option currently \
has no effect. Good:"
}], "ExampleText",
 CellID->50988412, ExpressionUUID->"4a45510a-f23a-814f-aa14-44845bc7b471"],

